From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FirebirdTA01 <FirebirdTA01@aol.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 17:39:26 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] spu: treat vector as a context-sensitive keyword under strict -std=

The system PS3 SPU compiler accepts vector (vector unsigned int ...) in
every language mode; upstream GCC enables the context-sensitive keyword only
when flag_iso is clear, so strict -std=c99/c++98 SPU code written against
that compiler fails with "vector does not name a type".

(Ported to GCC 16 from patches/spu/gcc-9.5.0/0009-spu-vector-keyword-strict-std.patch.)
---
 gcc/config/spu/spu-c.cc | 5 ++++-
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/gcc/config/spu/spu-c.cc b/gcc/config/spu/spu-c.cc
index 9009c004ac0..d9f25147d32 100644
--- a/gcc/config/spu/spu-c.cc
+++ b/gcc/config/spu/spu-c.cc
@@ -209,7 +209,10 @@ spu_cpu_cpp_builtins (struct cpp_reader *pfile)
        gcc_unreachable ();
     }
 
-  if (!flag_iso && cpp_get_options (pfile)->lang != CLK_ASM)
+  /* The PS3 reference SPU compiler recognises vector as a context-sensitive
+     keyword in every language mode, not only the GNU dialects, and code
+     written against its headers relies on it under a strict -std=.  */
+  if (cpp_get_options (pfile)->lang != CLK_ASM)
     {
       /* Define this when supporting context-sensitive keywords.  */
       cpp_define (pfile, "__VECTOR_KEYWORD_SUPPORTED__");
